#
# Keep e1000e out of PCI runtime power management.
#
# e1000e_pm_runtime_idle() arms a 5s suspend timer whenever the link is down.
# When the timer fires, __e1000_shutdown() has to reach the PHY - and on some
# PCH-integrated PHYs (I219 on 300-series and later) that access fails. The
# suspend callback then calls e1000e_pm_runtime_resume(), which brings the
# whole device back up, and returns -EBUSY. The device goes idle again, the
# idle callback re-arms the timer, and the cycle repeats indefinitely.
#
# The symptom is a permanently busy "kworker/N:M+pm" (the down/up cycle runs
# on pm_wq) alongside ksoftirqd/N (e1000e_up re-enables IRQs and NAPI each
# time round), plus a link that never settles.
#
# Setting power/control to "on" stops the PM core from ever attempting a
# runtime suspend, which breaks the cycle. Cost is a small amount of idle
# power on a NIC that is expected to stay up anyway. Hardware where runtime
# suspend works correctly loses nothing but that idle saving.
#

# Match on "bind" as well as "add": on "add" the driver is not necessarily
# bound yet, so DRIVER== can still be empty at that point. Matching the device
# itself (DRIVER) rather than the parent chain (DRIVERS) keeps this to the NIC.
ACTION=="add|bind", SUBSYSTEM=="pci", DRIVER=="e1000e", ATTR{power/control}="on"
